panics: panic

panic(v) throws a C++ exception (gp::panic_error) that carries the value. Deferred calls run
while it unwinds (gp_df.gp_take keeps it for gp_run; a panic statement in the function
itself does not even throw: gp_df.gp_panic and a goto), and recover can stop it. An
unrecovered panic prints Go's message ("panic: boom", or "panic: runtime error: index out of
range [5] with length 3", with the goroutine trace) and exits with status 2. The runtime's
own checks panic with Go's runtime.Error messages; a nil dereference also prints Go's signal
line.

Go's fatal errors (out of memory, stack overflow, all goroutines asleep) end the program the
same way, but no recover stops them and no deferred call runs.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#runtime-checks-and-panics
See also: gocpp explain defer, gocpp explain recover, gocpp explain checks
Also known as: panic, errors, exceptions

Example (panics.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    func main() {
    	defer fmt.Println("deferred calls run while a panic unwinds")
    	var m map[string]int
    	fmt.Println(m["a"])
    	panic("boom")
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/panics.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/panics.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./panics.gp:5: func main() {
    // gp[defer] col 1: this function has a defer: the body runs in try { } with a gp::defer_frame
    //     (gp_df); every exit goes through gp_df.gp_run(), which runs the deferred calls last in, first
    //     out, then rethrows a pending panic
    void main() {
        gp::defer_frame gp_df; try {

    // ---- ./panics.gp:6: defer fmt.Println("deferred calls run while a panic unwinds")
    // gp[defer] col 2: defer: the function and its arguments are evaluated now and stored in the frame;
    //     the call runs when the function exits: a builtin, a method or a package function: a lambda
    //     that makes the call, with the arguments as parameters (gp_push_call)
    // gp[interfaces] col 8: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
            gp_df.gp_push_call([](auto&&...) { gp::fmt::Println("deferred calls run while a panic unwinds"_gs); });

    // ---- ./panics.gp:7: var m map[string]int
            gp::map<gp::string, int64_t> m{};

    // ---- ./panics.gp:8: fmt.Println(m["a"])
    // gp[maps] col 15: m[k]: gp::get gives the zero value for a missing key and never inserts; a nil
    //     map reads as empty
            gp::fmt::Println(gp::get(m, "a"_gs));

    // ---- ./panics.gp:9: panic("boom")
    // gp[panics] col 2: panic: throws a C++ exception (gp::panic_error) that carries the value;
    //     deferred calls run while it unwinds, and recover can stop it; unrecovered, the runtime prints
    //     Go's message and trace and exits with status 2
            gp_df.gp_panic(gp::new_panic_constant("boom"_gs));
            goto gp_pan0;

    // ---- ./panics.gp:10: }
        } catch (gp::panic_error& gp_p) {
            gp_df.gp_take(gp_p);
        } catch (...) {
            gp_df.gp_adopt();
        }
        gp_pan0:; gp_df.gp_run();
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
